# Vigilant Vole API

> REST API of a Vigilant Vole uptime monitor (version <VERSION>), generated from the server's own route table. Everything the web app can do (monitors, tags, notification channels, status pages, incidents, maintenance, settings, configuration as code) can be done with these JSON endpoints.

- Instance: http://vv.test
- API base URL: http://vv.test/api/v1
- Browsable reference: http://vv.test/api-docs (sign in) · OpenAPI 3.1: http://vv.test/api/v1/openapi.json
- The web interface is translated; this document, field names, values and error codes are English.

Examples use two shell variables; never paste a real token into a chat or commit it:

```bash
export VV_URL="http://vv.test"
export VV_TOKEN="<API token from Settings → API tokens>"
curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $VV_TOKEN" "$VV_URL/api/v1/me"
```

## Authentication

- Create a token in the web app under **Settings → API tokens** (`/settings/tokens`). Tokens start with `vv_`, are shown once and can expire (30, 90, 365 days or never).
- Send it on every request: `Authorization: Bearer $VV_TOKEN`.
- Scope `read` acts as a **viewer** (every GET); scope `write` acts as the token's owner with their role.
- Roles: **viewer** reads everything; **editor** also changes monitors, tags, notification channels, status pages, incidents and maintenance and exports configuration; **admin** also changes settings, users, remote browsers, agents and imports configuration. Each endpoint below names the least role it needs.
- API tokens work only under `/api/` and for `/metrics`; they can't open the web interface.
- `/metrics` (Prometheus) takes the token as a Bearer token or as the HTTP Basic password (any user name).
- Machine credentials are separate: push monitors carry their own token in the URL, remote agents send an agent token (`vva_…`).

## Conventions

- JSON in and out; send bodies with `Content-Type: application/json`. `/api/v1/config` also speaks YAML.
- Lists return `{"data": [...], "next_cursor": "42" | null}`. Pass `?limit=` (1–500, default 50) and `?cursor=<next_cursor>` for the next page; stop when `next_cursor` is `null`.
- GETs send a weak `ETag` and answer `If-None-Match` with `304 Not Modified`.
- Times are UTC strings `YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS`.
- Secrets (passwords, webhook URLs, keys) are returned masked as `"••••••"`. Send `"••••••"` back (or leave the field out of a PATCH) to keep the stored value.
- `POST` that creates returns `201 Created` with a `Location` header; `DELETE` returns `204 No Content`.
- `PUT` replaces an object (fields left out go back to defaults); `PATCH` changes only the fields you send.
- Cookie-authenticated writes must come from the app's own pages; token requests are exempt.

## Errors

Every error uses one envelope (`fields` only on validation errors):

```json
{
  "error": {
    "code": "validation_failed",
    "message": "Enter a full URL, including https://, like https://example.com.",
    "fields": {"target": "Enter a full URL, including https://, like https://example.com."}
  }
}
```

| Code | HTTP | When |
|---|---|---|
| `bad_request` | 400 | malformed query, cursor or body |
| `unauthorized` | 401 | missing, invalid, expired or revoked token |
| `forbidden` | 403 | your role or the token's scope doesn't allow it |
| `not_found` | 404 | no such object or route |
| `conflict` | 409 | clashes with existing data or the object's state (duplicate tag name or slug, checking a paused monitor) |
| `too_large` | 413 | configuration document over 2 MB |
| `unsupported_media_type` | 415 | body without `Content-Type: application/json` |
| `validation_failed` | 422 | invalid fields; `fields` maps each field to its message |
| `invalid_document` | 422 | configuration document that can't be parsed |
| `delivery_failed` | 502 | a test notification the provider refused |
| `internal` | 500 | server error; try again |

## Monitor types

`monitor_type` is one of: `http`, `keyword`, `json_query`, `tcp`, `ping`, `tailscale_ping`, `dns`, `push`, `docker`, `websocket`, `domain_expiry`, `ntp`, `steam`, `game_server`, `tls`, `smtp`, `redis`, `postgres`, `mysql`, `mqtt`, `kafka`, `rabbitmq`, `grpc`, `grpc_keyword`, `radius`, `snmp`, `sqlserver`, `mongodb`, `real_browser`, `group`.

## Endpoints: Monitors

### GET /api/v1/monitors

List monitors with their current status.

- Access: viewer (any API token, read scope is enough)
- Success: `200 OK`
- Query parameters:
  - `status`: up | down | pending | maintenance | paused | unknown
  - `type`: monitor type, e.g. http
  - `tag`: tag name
  - `q`: search name, target and description
  - `limit`: page size (1–500, default 50)
  - `cursor`: next_cursor from the previous page

Example request:

```bash
curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $VV_TOKEN" "$VV_URL/api/v1/monitors"
```

Example response:

```json
{"data": [ …monitor… ], "next_cursor": "42"}
```

### POST /api/v1/monitors

Create a monitor. Same validation as the monitor form; unknown tag names are created.

- Access: editor (write token of an editor or admin)
- Success: `201 Created`
- Body: JSON object; fields used in the example: `escalation_minutes` (integer), `interval_seconds` (integer), `monitor_type` (string), `name` (string), `notifications` (array), `retries` (integer), `tags` (array), `target` (string)

Example request:

```bash
curl -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $VV_TOKEN" -H "Content-Type: application/json" "$VV_URL/api/v1/monitors" -d '{
  "name": "Website",
  "monitor_type": "http",
  "target": "https://example.com",
  "interval_seconds": 60,
  "retries": 1,
  "tags": ["production", {"name": "env", "value": "prod"}],
  "notifications": [1, {"channel_id": 3, "escalation": true}],
  "escalation_minutes": 15
}'
```

Example response:

```json
{
  "id": 7,
  "name": "Website",
  "description": "",
  "monitor_type": "http",
  "target": "https://example.com",
  "parent_id": null,
  "interval_seconds": 60,
  "retry_interval_seconds": null,
  "timeout_seconds": 10,
  "retries": 1,
  "active": true,
  "upside_down": false,
  "http_method": "GET",
  "accepted_status_codes": "200-399",
  "max_redirects": 10,
  "ignore_tls": false,
  "expiry_notify": true,
  "expiry_days": 14,
  "status": "up",
  "tags": [{"id": 2, "name": "production", "color": "green", "value": ""}],
  "notifications": [{"channel_id": 1, "escalation": false}],
  "created_at": "2026-10-19 08:00:00",
  "updated_at": "2026-10-19 08:00:00"
}
```

