```

Example response:

```json
{"ok": true}
```

### GET /api/badge/{id}/{kind}

SVG badge: kind is status, uptime, ping or cert-exp. Public when the monitor is on a published status page or badges are public.

- Access: public (no token)
- Success: `200 OK`
- Path parameters: `id` (integer), `kind` (string)
- Query parameters:
  - `duration`: 24h | 7d | 30d
  - `style`: flat | flat-square
  - `label`: custom label

Example request:

```bash
curl "$VV_URL/api/badge/7/status"
```

### GET /api/status-page/{slug}

A published status page as JSON (CORS enabled), for embedding.

- Access: public (no token)
- Success: `200 OK`
- Path parameters: `slug` (string)

Example request:

```bash
curl "$VV_URL/api/status-page/acme"
```

Example response:

```json
{"title": "Acme status", "overall": {"indicator": "operational", "title": "All systems operational"}, "sections": [ … ], "incidents": [ … ]}
```

### GET /api/agent/v1/monitors

For remote agents: the monitors to run (Bearer vva_… token, ETag).

- Access: remote agent token (`Authorization: Bearer vva_…`)
- Success: `200 OK`

Example request:

```bash
curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $VV_AGENT_TOKEN" "$VV_URL/api/agent/v1/monitors"
```

### POST /api/agent/v1/results

For remote agents: a batch of up to 500 results.

- Access: remote agent token (`Authorization: Bearer vva_…`)
- Success: `200 OK`
- Body: JSON object; fields used in the example: `results` (array)

Example request:

```bash
curl -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $VV_AGENT_TOKEN" -H "Content-Type: application/json" "$VV_URL/api/agent/v1/results" -d '{"results": [{"monitor_id": 7, "status": "up", "response_time_ms": 120, "message": "200 OK", "checked_at": "2026-10-19 14:03:00"}]}'
```

### GET /metrics

Prometheus metrics (when enabled). Bearer token or basic-auth password; Kuma-compatible series included.

- Access: API token as Bearer token or HTTP Basic password
- Success: `200 OK`

Example request:

```bash
curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $VV_TOKEN" "$VV_URL/metrics"
```

### POST /mcp

Model Context Protocol server (Streamable HTTP, JSON-RPC 2.0) for AI assistants: tools to read and, with a write token, steer your monitors, incidents and maintenance, plus this reference as a resource. Bearer API token; GET answers 405. See docs/mcp.md.

- Access: public (no token)
- Success: `200 OK`
- Body: JSON object; fields used in the example: `id` (integer), `jsonrpc` (string), `method` (string), `params` (object)

Example request:

```bash
curl -X POST -H "Content-Type: application/json" "$VV_URL/mcp" -d '{"jsonrpc": "2.0", "id": 1, "method": "tools/call", "params": {"name": "why_is_it_down", "arguments": {"id": 7}}}'
```

### GET /healthz

Liveness probe: always "ok".

- Access: public (no token)
- Success: `200 OK`

Example request:

```bash
curl "$VV_URL/healthz"
```

## Config as code

The whole setup (settings, tags, notification channels, monitors, status pages, maintenance) is one YAML or JSON document. Export needs an editor (secrets only for admins with `secrets=1`); import needs an admin. An import is one transaction: on any error you get `422` with every problem and nothing is written. Objects are matched by name (status pages by slug); masked secrets keep their stored values.

```bash
# Export (YAML; add format=json for JSON)
curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $VV_TOKEN" "$VV_URL/api/v1/config" -o vigilant.yml

# Preview what an import would change (nothing is written)
curl -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $VV_TOKEN" -H "Content-Type: application/yaml" \
  --data-binary @vigilant.yml "$VV_URL/api/v1/config?dry_run=1"

# Apply (merge: create and update, never delete)
curl -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $VV_TOKEN" -H "Content-Type: application/yaml" \
  --data-binary @vigilant.yml "$VV_URL/api/v1/config"

# Replace: also deletes what the document doesn't list (in the sections it contains); needs confirm=1
curl -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $VV_TOKEN" -H "Content-Type: application/yaml" \
  --data-binary @vigilant.yml "$VV_URL/api/v1/config?mode=replace&confirm=1"
```

## Common tasks

### Find monitors that are down

```bash
curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $VV_TOKEN" "$VV_URL/api/v1/monitors?status=down"
```

### Create an HTTP monitor

Tags are names (created if new) or `{"name", "value"}`; notifications are channel ids or `{"channel_id", "escalation"}` (list them with `GET /api/v1/notification-channels`).

```bash
curl -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $VV_TOKEN" -H "Content-Type: application/json" "$VV_URL/api/v1/monitors" -d '{
  "name": "Website",
  "monitor_type": "http",
  "target": "https://example.com",
  "interval_seconds": 60,
  "retries": 1,
  "tags": ["production", {"name": "env", "value": "prod"}],
  "notifications": [1, {"channel_id": 3, "escalation": true}],
  "escalation_minutes": 15
}'
```

### Pause and resume a monitor

```bash
curl -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $VV_TOKEN" "$VV_URL/api/v1/monitors/7/pause"
curl -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $VV_TOKEN" "$VV_URL/api/v1/monitors/7/resume"
```

### Bulk edit: run on remote agents, change the interval

Pick monitors with `ids` or `filter` (the list's filters: `status`, `type`, `tag`, `q`; `{}` is every monitor). Every key of `set` is optional (left out or `null` keeps each monitor's value): `run_on_agents`, `confirm_with_agents` (booleans); `interval_seconds`, `retry_interval_seconds`, `timeout_seconds`, `retries` (integers); `channels` (`{"add": [...], "remove": [...]}` or `{"replace": [...]}`, entries are ids, names or `{"id"|"name", "escalation"}`); `tags` (`{"add": [...], "remove": [...], "clear": true}`). Monitors a setting doesn't apply to come back in `skipped` with a reason.

```bash
curl -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $VV_TOKEN" -H "Content-Type: application/json" "$VV_URL/api/v1/monitors/bulk" -d '{"filter": {"type": "http", "tag": "production"}, "set": {"run_on_agents": true, "confirm_with_agents": true, "interval_seconds": 120, "channels": {"add": [{"name": "Ops Discord", "escalation": false}], "remove": [3]}, "tags": {"add": [{"name": "eu", "color": "teal", "value": "west"}], "remove": ["legacy"]}}}'
```

### Create a status page

Then publish it and change its look with `PATCH /api/v1/status-pages/{id}`; it is served at `/status/<slug>`.

```bash
curl -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $VV_TOKEN" -H "Content-Type: application/json" "$VV_URL/api/v1/status-pages" -d '{"title": "Acme status", "slug": "acme", "sections": [{"name": "API", "monitor_ids": [7, 8]}]}'
```
